1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
During an outage, incident response often stalls outside the system.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:12,500
An operator stops diagnosing, waits on vendor support, repeats context, and manually carries the ticket, status, and ETA back into the incident workflow.

3
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:18,000
IncidentBridge delegates exactly that bounded phone job to CALL-E.

4
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:24,000
The core rule is simple: a phone call may create evidence, but it never gets recovery authority.

5
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:30,700
The workflow validates the briefing, previews the exact task, authorizes one destination, reserves before dispatch, then binds and corroborates the result.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,500
The public console clearly labels what is real, what is previewed, and what is simulated.

7
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:44,600
This is a real CALL-E provider run. The recipient was unavailable, so IncidentBridge failed closed to needs human, kept incident closed false, and made no automatic retry.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,500
The preview path creates no call and exposes the exact task, schema, destination mask, and decision boundary.

9
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:56,800
Live execution still requires explicit intent, recipient authorization, an exact E.164 allowlist match, and the server-side credential.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,500
The acknowledged path captures a support ticket, vendor status, ETA, workaround, and callback information.

11
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:08,800
Even on that success path, incident closed remains false until local checks and the incident commander verify recovery.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
CALL-E maintainers then reviewed IncidentBridge in the official awesome-phone-call-agents repository.

13
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:27,800
They found two substantive safety blockers: incomplete privacy validation and insufficient result binding and transcript corroboration. Both were fixed, regression-tested, and PR number one thirty-two was merged.

14
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Current GitHub Actions verification is twenty-one passing tests at ninety-two point three seven percent coverage, with a ninety percent gate.

15
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:46,800
The integration suite runs the published CALL-E Python SDK through loopback HTTP and verifies the real create-call request, bearer auth, idempotency header, strict result schema, and polling path.

16
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:53,500
For real-world impact, IncidentBridge avoids inventing an MTTR or revenue number.

17
00:01:53,620 --> 00:02:00,500
The calculator lets a team enter its own vendor-call volume, manual operator minutes, review burden, and delegation share.

18
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:09,000
That yields one auditable business metric: operator hours returned to incident work. The paired safety metrics remain unauthorized calls and false successes, both targeted at zero.
